<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia</h1><p class="st-meta">1860 CE – 1862 CE · capital Perquenco</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom proclaimed among the Mapuche by a French adventurer in 1860, which never won recognition but survives as a claim of French pretenders.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orelie-antoine-de-tounens">Orélie-Antoine de Tounens</a>, a lawyer from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dordogne">Dordogne</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, travelled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> in 1858 and went to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/araucania">Araucanía</a>, the independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapuche">Mapuche</a> lands south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biobio-river">Biobío</a>. In November 1860 he proclaimed himself King Orélie-Antoine I of Araucanía and, days later, added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patagonia">Patagonia</a>, claiming support from Mapuche chiefs.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom existed largely on paper. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orelie-antoine-de-tounens">Orélie-Antoine</a> issued a constitution, a flag and decrees, and some <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/araucania">Mapuche</a> leaders, such as Quilapán, seem to have seen him as a potential ally against Chilean expansion. He had no army, administration or international recognition, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> regarded the scheme as a threat to its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chilean authorities arrested him in January 1862, declared him insane and deported him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He returned several times without success and died in France in 1878. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> carried out the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/occupation-of-araucania">Occupation of Araucanía</a> by 1883. French claimants still use his title.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-araucania-and-patagonia.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: B1mbo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Kingdom_of_Araucan%C3%ADa_and_Patagonia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1860 CE – 1862 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Perquenco</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Orélie-Antoine de Tounens</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism, Mapuche religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mapudungun, French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/chile">Chile</a>, <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Araucanía and Patagonia, Royaume d'Araucanie et de Patagonie</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1861&amp;focus=kingdom-of-araucania-and-patagonia" aria-label="Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2881 3557 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Araucanía and Patagonia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3201 3813L3195 3647"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3195 3647h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3201 3813h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3201 3813h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Perquenco. <a href="/atlas?year=1861&amp;focus=kingdom-of-araucania-and-patagonia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

